  <h1>LLM Use-Cases in HFT &amp; Quant Firms</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: an LLM has already shipped
    code into this repo — your latest merged PR is literally
    <code>Claude_Fix_Ups_and_Documentations</code>. The interesting question isn't
    "should we use LLMs?" but "where in a trading firm does an LLM <em>actually</em>
    earn its keep, and where is using one a category error?"
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 THE HONEST MAP ===================== -->
  <h2>1. The honest map: LLMs are everywhere <em>except</em> the hot path</h2>

  <p>
    Lesson 0007 introduced transformers (BERT, FinBERT). Large Language Models —
    GPT-class, Claude-class, Llama-class — are the same architecture scaled to
    10⁹–10¹² parameters and pretrained on internet-scale text plus code. They
    are the most general-purpose tool in your toolbox and the slowest. Two
    consequences shape every honest use case:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>They cannot trade tick-to-trade.</strong> A single LLM call is 100ms–10s. The FPGA budget (Lessons 0001, 0002, 0004) is nanoseconds. Three orders of magnitude past the wall, even on the fastest hosted models.</li>
    <li><strong>They are excellent at unstructured-to-structured.</strong> Free text → JSON, news article → sentiment + entities + event type, codebase → summary, log file → root-cause hypothesis. <em>That's</em> the alpha.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>So they cluster in three layers of a trading firm, all <em>off</em> the hot path:</p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Research &amp; dev <span class="tag fit">strong fit</span></h4>
      <p>Code generation, refactoring, research scripts, paper Q&amp;A, exploration of new data sources. Hours of dev time saved per engineer per week.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Signal generation (offline) <span class="tag fit">strong fit</span></h4>
      <p>Earnings calls, 10-K filings, news, social media. Turn unstructured text into structured features that feed the next-day GBM. This is where revenue impact is biggest.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Ops, compliance, support <span class="tag fit">strong fit</span></h4>
      <p>Compliance surveillance of trader chats, incident postmortems, document generation, customer/IR Q&amp;A. Boring but billed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Where they don't fit:</strong> the FPGA hot path; per-tick decisions of
    any kind; market-making quote logic; live exchange routing; anything that needs
    a sub-millisecond response. Don't try to be clever here — physics wins.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 SEVEN USE CASES ===================== -->
  <h2>2. Seven concrete use cases, grounded in this codebase</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>1. Research code-gen and refactoring <span class="tag fit">used in this repo</span></h4>
      <p>
        Engineers describe a task in natural language; the LLM produces a diff. Your
        own <code>git log</code> shows it: merge <code>afeb129</code> is
        <code>Claude_Fix_Ups_and_Documentations</code>. The repo is being maintained
        partly by an LLM right now. Industry: nearly universal in 2025.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>2. News/filing &amp; earnings-call NLP <span class="tag fit">highest revenue impact</span></h4>
      <p>
        The natural extension of <code>news_features.py</code>. Today Alpha Vantage
        gives you one scalar sentiment number per article. An LLM can extract: event
        type ("guidance raise"), magnitude ("+3%"), entities ("CEO leaving"),
        forward-looking statements — all as structured JSON that becomes new GBM
        features. This is where buy-side desks see real alpha.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>3. Document &amp; report generation <span class="tag fit">used in this repo</span></h4>
      <p>
        <code>docs/generate_docs.py</code> builds <code>DEVELOPER_GUIDE.docx</code>
        from Markdown. A small LLM pass can summarize, audit, or expand sections,
        regenerating prose around your hard-coded structure. Production HFT firms use
        LLMs to draft client reports, factor-model commentary, monthly attribution.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>4. Codebase &amp; data-room Q&amp;A <span class="tag fit">workhorse</span></h4>
      <p>
        Index the codebase + your <code>docs/</code> + recent commits + Jira tickets
        into a vector store; ask "why does <code>walkforward_sim.py</code> have an HMM
        overlay?" and get an answer with citations. Eliminates the "ask the one person
        who knows" bottleneck. The lessons in <code>docs/learning/</code> you've been
        building are a textbook input corpus for exactly this.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>5. Compliance &amp; trade surveillance <span class="tag fit">regulated</span></h4>
      <p>
        Read trader Slack/Bloomberg chats, flag potential market manipulation, frontrunning
        language, MNPI leakage. Trad ML used keyword rules; LLMs grasp paraphrase
        and context. Highly regulated; usually self-hosted models for privacy.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>6. Strategy-code review &amp; test gen <span class="tag warn">careful</span></h4>
      <p>
        Generate unit tests for <code>paper_trading_sim.py:build_features</code>,
        spot look-ahead-bias bugs, audit risk constraints. Useful but unreliable —
        LLMs miss subtle bugs (or invent them); always have a human review the
        review.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>7. Alpha-GPT-style prompt-engineered signal mining <span class="tag warn">research</span></h4>
      <p>
        Recent (2024–2025) papers use LLMs to <em>propose</em> trading signals as
        Python code, which are then backtested. Promising as an exploration loop;
        productionizing the LLM's outputs requires the same walk-forward / leakage
        discipline as any other signal source.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>The thing the press loves but you shouldn't try: LLM-as-trader <span class="tag bad">don't</span></h4>
      <p>
        Asking GPT/Claude to "trade my account" directly. Benchmarks (StockBench,
        Oct 2025) show most LLM agents underperform buy-and-hold, and even the best
        struggle on risk management. Use LLMs to <em>build</em> trading code, not
        to <em>be</em> the trader.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §3 LATENCY WALL ===================== -->
  <h2>3. The latency wall — why LLMs can't sit on the hot path</h2>

  <p>
    A reminder from Lessons 0002 and 0004: HFT latency budgets are unforgiving.
    Map an LLM call onto them:
  </p>

  <div class="latency-wall">
    <div class="lat-row fast"><span class="lat-name">FPGA tick-to-trade</span><span>your <code>market_maker.vhd</code> ballpark</span><span class="lat-num">~100 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row fast"><span class="lat-name">Kernel-bypass CPU</span><span>Solarflare OpenOnload C++</span><span class="lat-num">~750 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row medium"><span class="lat-name">Linux kernel network</span><span>your FastAPI HTTP roundtrip</span><span class="lat-num">~50 µs</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row medium"><span class="lat-name">GBM inference (Python)</span><span><code>predict_next.py</code></span><span class="lat-num">~1 ms</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row slow"><span class="lat-name">Small LLM first token</span><span>~7B model on a GPU, cached</span><span class="lat-num">~100 ms</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row slow"><span class="lat-name">Frontier LLM full reply</span><span>GPT-5 / Claude Opus over HTTP</span><span class="lat-num">~1–10 s</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A single LLM call is roughly <strong>10⁷ to 10⁸ times slower</strong> than the
    arithmetic in your FPGA. There is no architecture, no quantization, no
    distillation trick that closes that gap by the right number of orders of
    magnitude while preserving useful LLM behavior. So:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hot path:</strong> never. Make decisions in nanoseconds; LLMs need milliseconds.</li>
    <li><strong>Warm path (intraday research, alerts):</strong> sometimes. Seconds are fine when you're rebalancing daily anyway.</li>
    <li><strong>Cold path (overnight research, reports, code):</strong> obviously. Latency irrelevant; quality is the only metric.</li>
  </ul>

