    for ops.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §5 PATTERNS ===================== -->
  <h2>5. Architecture patterns you'll see in any production LLM-in-quant setup</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)</h4>
      <p>
        Embed your corpus (filings, news, codebase, internal wiki), retrieve the
        top-K relevant chunks at query time, stuff them into the LLM's context
        window. Cheap, deterministic-ish, no fine-tuning. The default for
        codebase Q&amp;A and internal knowledge assistants.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Fine-tuning</h4>
      <p>
        Continue training a base model on your specific text (earnings calls,
        broker reports). Higher fixed cost, better domain-specific behavior, used
        when you have lots of high-quality labeled examples. FinBERT is a
        non-LLM analog you saw in Lesson 0007.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Tool use / function calling (incl. MCP)</h4>
      <p>
        The LLM doesn't compute Sharpe ratios — it calls
        <code>analyze_equity.compute_metrics()</code> with the right arguments and
        formats the answer. Model Context Protocol (MCP) is the 2024–2025
        standard for exposing tools to LLMs. This pattern is how an LLM safely
        interacts with your <em>real</em> code.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Agents (multi-step, autonomous)</h4>
      <p>
        LLM plans → executes a tool → reads result → plans next step. Useful for
        open-ended research ("find me a momentum signal that survives 2018–2024
        walk-forward"). Hard to make robust; failure modes include infinite loops
        and confident wrongness.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Self-hosted vs hosted</h4>
      <p>
        Quant firms with sensitive data lean self-hosted (Llama, Mistral,
        Qwen, DeepSeek) on internal GPU clusters. For non-sensitive workloads
        (this repo's CLAUDE.md Q&amp;A), hosted APIs are cheaper and capable
        enough.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Distillation</h4>
      <p>
        Train a small model to mimic the input/output pairs of a frontier model.
        Get most of the quality at a fraction of the cost. Standard for high-volume
        feature-extraction jobs like "score every news article from 2010–2025."
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §6 CONTRASTS ===================== -->
  <h2>6. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>LLM vs FinBERT (and other small NNs)</h4>
      <p>
        Both are transformers. <strong>FinBERT</strong> ≈ 100M params, fine-tuned
        for one task (sentiment classification), runs cheaply.
        <strong>LLM</strong> = 10⁹–10¹² params, general-purpose, expensive per
        call. Use FinBERT for one repeatable task; use an LLM for variable,
        open-ended tasks.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>LLM-as-tool vs LLM-as-trader</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>LLM-as-tool</strong>: helps engineers write code, summarize
        research, extract features. Boring, valuable, widely deployed.
        <strong>LLM-as-trader</strong>: lets the LLM place orders directly.
        Demoed in papers; underperforms buy-and-hold; not used in production.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>RAG vs Fine-tuning</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>RAG</strong>: change what's in context per query. No training.
        Updates instantly when your docs change. <strong>Fine-tuning</strong>:
        change the model's weights. Better fluency on your domain. Slower to
        update. Hybrid is common; RAG-first is the default for codebase Q&amp;A.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Embedding vs Completion</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Embedding</strong> models turn text into a vector for similarity
        search (the retrieval half of RAG). <strong>Completion</strong> models
        generate text given a prompt. Same transformer ancestors; different
        endpoints and pricing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Hallucination vs Wrong-but-confident output</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Hallucination</strong>: model fabricates a fact (a paper that
        doesn't exist, a function that isn't in your code).
        <strong>Wrong-but-confident</strong>: model recalls a real fact incorrectly.
        Both look the same to the user; both demand a verification step before
        anything ships.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>System prompt vs User prompt</h4>
      <p>
        The <strong>system prompt</strong> is the persistent instruction set
        ("you are a quant research assistant; cite sources"). The <strong>user
        prompt</strong> is the per-query message. Mixing them up causes role
        confusion and weakens guardrails.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

