  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q4. You use a frontier LLM to extract structured features from each article in your news corpus and re-run after a month. Some article scores have drifted slightly even though the article text is identical. What's the most likely cause and clean fix?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="It can happen but it's typically not the cause of drift on identical inputs. The more common cause is model-side: provider updated the model, or your call used non-zero temperature.">Your CSV got corrupted in storage; restore from backup.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. Frontier hosted models update silently and decoding is stochastic by default. Pin the model version (e.g. claude-opus-4-7), set temperature=0, and cache outputs keyed on (model_id, prompt_hash). Now your feature pipeline is reproducible.">The hosted model was updated and/or your decoding has nonzero temperature; pin the model version, set temperature=0, and cache outputs by (model_id, prompt_hash) for reproducibility.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="LLMs can give consistent outputs at temperature=0 with a pinned model. Drift is a symptom of inadequate pinning, not an intrinsic property.">LLMs are intrinsically non-deterministic; there's nothing you can do.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That's a real bug source on Alpha Vantage's side, but here the inputs are unchanged. The drift is on the LLM extraction step, not on the source data.">Alpha Vantage rewrote the article text without telling you.</li>
