  <!-- ===================== §8 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>8. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. You want to add a "ask the codebase" feature to <code>webapp/main.py</code>. What does the minimum-viable plumbing look like, and what's the single biggest correctness concern?</summary>
    <p>
      Minimum-viable: chunk every <code>.py</code>, <code>.vhd</code>, and Markdown file into ~500-token
      chunks; embed each chunk into a vector with a hosted embeddings model; store
      vectors in something simple (FAISS in-process, or sqlite with cosine
      similarity). On query: embed the question, retrieve top-K chunks, build a
      prompt of the form <code>"Given the following code excerpts, answer the
      question. Cite filenames. ... {chunks} ... Question: {q}"</code>, return.
      Single biggest correctness concern: <strong>citations must be verifiable</strong> —
      every claim should point to a chunk that actually exists and actually
      supports the claim. Hallucinated citations are worse than no answer, because
      they look authoritative. Have the LLM emit
      <code>(filename, line range)</code> tuples that you re-fetch and display alongside its
      answer.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. Your news LLM pipeline costs $0.04 per article. You want to backfill 10 years of S&amp;P 500 news (~5M articles). Decision time. What are your options?</summary>
    <p>
      $0.04 × 5M = $200k for a one-time backfill with the frontier model — high but
      bounded; CFO might still sign off if alpha case is strong. Better options:
      <strong>(a) Distill</strong> a small open-source model (Llama-3, Qwen-2.5)
      using a few thousand labeled examples from the frontier model — call the
      frontier on 10k articles ($400), train a 7B distillate, run it on the
      remaining 4.99M at ~$0.0005/article using your own GPUs ($2,500). About
      ~$3k total instead of $200k. <strong>(b) Tiered processing</strong> —
      send only high-relevance-score articles to the frontier LLM; cheap model for
      the rest. <strong>(c) Sample-then-evaluate</strong> — process 50k random
      articles fully, measure feature-importance gain, only spend more if it
      moves the GBM's out-of-sample R². The error you want to avoid is committing
      the full $200k before proving any alpha.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. A regulator audits your firm and asks "did an LLM ever decide a trade?" How should the recordkeeping in this codebase be set up so you can answer truthfully?</summary>
    <p>
      The clean answer is: an LLM produces a <em>feature</em> that feeds the GBM;
      the GBM produces the prediction; an explicit allocation function produces
      the weights; an executor places the orders. Each step is auditable on its own.
      Practical recordkeeping: pin all LLM model IDs and prompts in version
      control; store every LLM call's <code>(input_hash, output, model_id, ts)</code>
      tuple in a write-once log; never wire an LLM's output directly into an order
      router. Then "did an LLM decide a trade?" honestly answers as "no — an LLM
      generated a feature value that contributed to a downstream model's prediction;
      here's the full chain." This separation is also a defense against a future
      regulator who categorically restricts "AI making trading decisions."
    </p>
  </details>

